Topic 3. 2.4 Spyware and Bloatware

Spyware — the hidden camera. Spyware sits quietly on a device and watches what you do, then sends it back to someone else. Think of a hidden camera in a rental property: you go about your day normally with no idea anyone is watching. It can collect: 
Browsing history and searches. Keystrokes (overlapping with the keyloggers 
Login details, banking info, location, even screenshots or camera/mic access

How it gets in: usually bundled with something you chose to install, like a “free” app, a fake toolbar or a dodgy browser extension, or delivered through phishing. It’s often hidden in the fine print of terms and conditions nobody reads. This is the "free couch" on the side walk(infected with flees or who knows what) all critical thinking goes out the window when something’s free.

Bloatware — the junk mail that came with the house
Bloatware is software that’s pre-installed on a new device by the manufacturer or phone carrier: trial antivirus, games, “helper” apps you never asked for. Imagine buying a house and finding the previous owner left furniture in every room. It’s not necessarily harmful, but it takes up space, and some of it might have problems you don’t know about.

Why bloatware is a security issue:
It expands the attack surface (remember 2.2): more software means more possible vulnerabilities
It’s often not updated or patched, because nobody uses it or knows it’s there
Some have genuine security holes or quietly collected data
It wastes storage, memory and performance

Defences:
Antivirus/anti-malware and EDR (spyware)
Be careful what you install; read permissions and check where downloads come from
Remove bloatware you don’t need, and build devices from a clean, standard image in business settings
Keep everything patched

What spyware is and how it usually ends up on a device?
What it collects: keystrokes, screenshots, camera and mic. All correct.
How it gets in: bundled with free apps, malicious apps even in official stores, email attachments, and drive-by downloads, where just visiting a compromised website can infect you with no click. 
The security concept that applies is least privilege: an app should only have the permissions it actually needs. A torch app asking for your contacts and microphone is a red flag. Reviewing app permissions regularly is a solid defence against both spyware and over-collecting legitimate apps.
Why bloatware is a security risk even if it’s not malicious?

How you’d protect against each one?
Attack surface: every extra app is another door.
Unpatched: nobody maintains it, so vulnerabilities never get fixed.
Unknown permissions: it may have access to things you’d never knowingly grant.
Wasted resources: storage, memory, performance.
It’s trusted by default: because it came with the device, people assume it’s fine.

The issue “you can’t defend against something you don’t even know about” is the key idea, and it’s a real security principle: asset inventory. Organisations keep a list of every device and piece of software they have, because unknown software can’t be patched, monitored or secured. 
Regularly reviewing apps and permissions and removing what you don’t use. To round it out:

Bloatware - Junk Mail the game that came with your phone that you forgot about(forgot its using memory, cant patch it, has permission to your camera)
Uninstall or disable what you don’t need
In business: build devices from a clean, standard image instead of using the manufacturer’s setup, and use MDM (mobile device management) to control which apps are allowed
