What’s the difference between DNS poisoning and domain hijacking? Who or what is actually being tampered with in each?

DNS poisoning = someone scribbles a wrong number on your personal copy of the phone book. The official directory at the city hall is still correct. When you tear out that page and get a fresh copy, or the TTL runs out the problem is gone. What's tampered: the resolver's in-memory cache entry. A temporary, local copy.
DNS cache: temporary, remembered answers. Your computer (and DNS servers) keep recent lookups for a while. These do expire when the TTL runs out, or you can flush them (ipconfig /flushdns). Hosts file: a plain text file sitting on the computer (C:\Windows\System32\drivers\etc\hosts). It’s checked before DNS, and it doesn’t expire. Flushing the cache won’t touch it. If an attacker edits it, the redirect stays until someone notices and fixes the file. Changing the client’s hosts file (affects one computer) an on-path attacker sitting in the middle and answering DNS queries with fake replies
poisoning the DNS server’s cache, which is the big one. Every user who asks that server gets the bad answer until the TTL expires.

Domain hijacking = is when the attacker makes a change to the registry on the DNS server. Someone breaks into the phone book and changes the contacts. Every copy of the phone book in the city is now wrong. You have to get into phone book fixed. 
Poisoning isn’t only on the client’s machine. Domain hijacking: One small terminology note: attackers usually break into the registrar account, meaning the company the domain was bought from, often with stolen login details. From there they can repoint the domain’s name servers or records. Because they’re changing the authoritative source, it affects everyone in the world, and it lasts until the real owner gets control back. No TTL will save you there.
Changing the client’s hosts file (affects one computer) an on-path attacker sitting in the middle and answering DNS queries with fake replies
poisoning the DNS server’s cache, which is the big one. Every user who asks that server gets the bad answer until the TTL expires.

Why is an attack on the DNS cache Server worse than just one computer? The blast radius for the Server is bigger, more than one computer is going to these web sites. A server’s cache still has TTLs. A cache is a cache, wherever it lives. DNS servers cache answers with a TTL just like your computer does, so a poisoned entry on a server will expire when its TTL runs out. (Attackers try to set a very long TTL on their fake record, and they can re-poison it, but it’s still temporary.) The server’s admins can also flush its cache once they spot the problem.
                              Reach (blast radius)         	          How long it lasts
Hosts file edit	          One computer	                    Persistent: no TTL, stays until someone finds and fixes the file
Server cache poisoning	Everyone using that server	          Temporary: expires with TTL or when admins flush it
Domain hijacking	          Everyone, worldwide                    	Until the real owner regains control of the registrar account

The last row is where it “lasts until they figure out it’s compromised” idea really belongs.
Memory Point:Poisoning corrupts a copy of the answer (on your machine, in transit, or in a server’s cache); hijacking takes over the original.
DNSSEC stops: forged answers in transit and poisoned server caches
DNSSEC doesn’t stop: hosts file edits (DNS is never asked) or domain hijacking (the attacker controls the real keys)
