Topic 9 Wireless Attacks 2.4 #9: On-path attacks
The idea: an attacker places themselves between two parties who think they’re talking directly to each other. Every message passes through the attacker, who can read it, change it, or pass it along untouched so nobody notices. It used to be called “man-in-the-middle”; on-path is the newer, neutral term.
Analogy: you and your bank post letters to each other, but someone has quietly redirected your mail to their own address first. They steam each envelope open, read it, maybe change “pay $50” to “pay $500”, reseal it and post it on. You and the bank both think the letters came straight from each other.

How attackers get into the middle:
ARP poisoning: on a local network, devices use ARP to match an IP address to a physical MAC address. ARP has no verification, so an attacker sends fake replies saying “the router’s IP is at my MAC address.” Your traffic to the router then goes to the attacker first. This only works on the same local network segment.
On-path browser attack (man-in-the-browser): malware sits inside your browser, so it sees everything after decryption. HTTPS doesn’t help, because the attacker is already on your end of the encrypted tunnel. This is common with banking trojans.

