What’s different about an on-path browser attack, and why doesn’t HTTPS protect you from it?
The difference is where the attacker sits relative to the encryption boundary
Why HTTPS can't help?
HTTPS has exactly one job: prove to the browser that the server is who it claims to be, and encrypt the transit. Once the browser has verified the certificate and decrypted the payload, its job is done. It has no mechanism to ask "is the code I'm about to hand this plaintext to actually the legitimate browser, or is there a malicious extension/binary also listening?"
HTTPS draws the line at the process boundary. An on-path browser attacker is on the right side of that line, so the encryption is irrelevant — it's like locking your front door when the burglar is already in your kitchen. HTTPS (using TLS) that proves the server’s identity, not HTTP. The server shows a certificate, the browser checks it, and then the traffic is encrypted in transit. Once the data arrives, the browser decrypts it so it can show you the page.
TLS is a locked room that the data passes through on its way to your browser.

On-path browser attack (man-in-the-browser)
HTTPS does a great job on the network. The server shows its certificate, my browser checks it’s genuine, and everything travelling between us is encrypted. Anyone sitting on the network in between just sees scrambled data. But once that data reaches my browser, it has to be decrypted so I can actually see the page. That’s where HTTPS’s job ends. I think of TLS as the lock on my front door. If a burglar is outside, the lock keeps them out. But with a man-in-the-browser attack, the burglar is already inside. They’re hiding in my pantry, eating my cookies, and listening to everything said at the kitchen table. Muttley doesn’t even bark. That’s what makes this attack so dangerous: nothing looks wrong. The padlock is still in the address bar, the certificate is real, and my bank’s website looks exactly the same as always. The malware sits inside the browser, so it sees everything in plain text: the page after it’s decrypted and everything I type before it’s encrypted and sent. It doesn’t just listen, either. It can swap the notes. I type “pay $50 to Bob,” the malware quietly changes it to “pay $5,000 to the attacker,” and my screen still shows $50.

How the burglar gets in: banking trojans (Zeus is the classic example), malicious browser extensions, or malware picked up from a phishing email.
